jank-32: untyped params speculated as f64 too: an f64 entry beside 28's i64 entry and the generic
body, chosen per call by each argument's tag
(cohost doc/upstream/jank-32-real-speculation.md; lane U6, order U6-4)

Base: jank-lang/jank f3d82d65499920f81216e9235bd5d3a34a968e3f with the series before it in
upstream/jank/patches/series (11, 13, 14, 12-21, 18, 19, 15a-16, 15b-17, 22, 20, 23, 24, 26, 25, 27,
28). Apply from the clone's root: git apply <this file>.

Builds on 28-param-speculation (an i64 native entry beside the generic body).

Files:
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/analyze/processor.cpp, include .../analyze/processor.hpp,
  .../analyze/expr/function.hpp:
  - real_speculation_candidate: an arity whose body has a real literal, a `/`, or a Math or cpp
    symbol is also speculated as f64 (analyze_fn_arity's speculate_params 2).
  - integer_params: in the f64 speculation an untyped param stays i64 when the body shows integer
    evidence for it (an operand of inc, dec, quot, rem, mod, nth, get, range, a bit op, or of
    arithmetic or a comparison against an integer literal or a let/loop local initialized from one);
    every other untyped param is f64. So an nbody step (an integer count, real coordinates) gets
    the entry its calls need. An f64 speculation with no f64 param is not tried (it is 28's).
  - with_speculation: tries i64 (28) and f64; with both, the i64 arity is the main one and the f64
    one its alternate (function_arity::alt, fn_ctx->alt_real); with one, it is the main one. The
    generic twin is kept either way.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/ir/{processor,builder}.cpp, include .../ir/{builder,instruction}.hpp:
  the alternate is an IR function <name>_<n>_f64, emitted like the generic twin; named recursion
  inside it calls <name>_<n>_f64_native (inst::named_recursion::alt_real).
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/codegen/cpp_processor.cpp: the alternate's native entry is
  <name>_<n>_f64_native and it has no boxing entry. The main boxing entry checks each speculated
  argument against its own param's kind (a tagged small integer or integer object for an i64 param;
  a tagged small real or real object for an f64 param), calls the main native entry, then the f64
  alternate, then the generic body. An integer is never converted to a real: (scale 2) with
  (defn scale [x] (* x 1.5)) takes the generic body and returns Clojure's 3.0.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/ir/processor.cpp, gen(let): a loop's recur shadows are scoped to
  the loop, as its locals are (saved and restored around it). Found by bench:programs: an inner
  loop that rebinds an outer loop's name ((loop [y 0 n 0] ... (recur (inc y) (loop [x 0 n n] ...))))
  is generated as an argument of the outer recur and used to leave its own shadow in the map, so
  the outer recur assigned to the inner loop's out-of-scope shadow: C++ "use of undeclared
  identifier 'sNN'" (on the apt binary too). This is why mandelbrot, fannkuch and nsieve (and
  nbody on cohost) were ABSENT on every jank target. Independent of the f64 work; it can be split
  into its own patch.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/analyze/processor.cpp, analyze_if: two integral branches whose
  common type is unsigned (a native i64 and a count's size_t) give an i64 (Clojure's integers are
  signed). Found by bench:programs' AOT harness, (defn __sinkv [acc r] (+ acc (if (number? r) r
  (count (str r))))): speculated (28), r = -502 became 2^64-502 in the unsigned if and the checked
  add threw "integer overflow". Repro 32's "if signedness" line.

